\id 2TI - Restored Context Bible (RCB)
\usfm 3.0
\ide UTF-8
\h 2 Timothy
\toc1 The Second Letter of Paul to Timothy
\toc2 2 Timothy
\toc3 2Ti
\mt1 2 Timothy

\imt Introduction
\ip This letter comes from Paul, in prison in Rome and expecting to die there (1:8, 16-17; 2:9; 4:6), to Timothy, his younger colleague, stationed at Ephesus in the Roman province of Asia. The date is the middle of the first century.
\ip Everything has gone wrong since Paul's first letter to Timothy. Then Paul was travelling and expected to be back in Ephesus shortly. Now he is in a confinement severe enough that a friend arriving from Asia had to hunt through Rome to find him (1:17). He has stood trial once and no one appeared in court on his side (4:16). Almost everyone in Asia has cut ties with him (1:15) — association with a condemned prisoner had become too costly — and Demas, one of his own circle, has walked out (4:10). Only Luke is still with him. He is cold and wants his cloak before winter closes the shipping lanes (4:13, 21).
\ip Meanwhile the false teaching that provoked the first letter has spread rather than subsided. Hymenaeus, disciplined once already, is back with a partner, teaching that the raising of the dead is a thing already past, and people's faith is collapsing under it (2:17-18).
\ip So this is a last letter, of a kind Israel's readers knew well — the dying man's charge to his successor, as Jacob charged his sons and Moses charged Joshua. Paul reviews what his own life came to, warns of what is coming, and hands over the work. Its single concern is that what he was given should survive him: guard it, suffer for it, and put it into the hands of people who will hand it on again (2:2).

\c 1
\s1 Greeting
\p
\v 1 Paul, one sent\f + \fr 1:1 \ft Greek \tl apostolos\tl*: an emissary who carries the full authority of the one who sent him.\f* of the Christ Jesus through the will of God, in line with the promise of life\f + \fr 1:1 \ft Greek \tl epangelia\tl*, the word for God's sworn undertakings to Israel — to Abraham, to David, through the prophets. What was promised was life: "Choose life, that you and your offspring may live" (Deuteronomy 30:19); "many who sleep in the dust of the earth shall awake" (Daniel 12:2).\f* that is in the Christ Jesus,
\v 2 to Timothy, a beloved child \add [i.e. my own son in this work, though not by blood]\add*: favor, mercy, peace\f + \fr 1:2 \ft Greek \tl charis\tl*, \tl eleos\tl*, \tl eirēnē\tl* — free good-will, covenant kindness shown to those in need, and \tl shalom\tl*, which is not the absence of conflict but wholeness. Paul's usual greeting is two-part; mercy is added in both letters to Timothy, and this one keeps returning to it (1:16, 18).\f* from God the Father and the Christ Jesus our Lord.
\s1 Thanking God for the Faith That Came Down to You
\p
\v 3 I am grateful to God, whom I serve as my forebears did \add [i.e. the God of my fathers, the same God Israel has always served — I have not gone over to another]\add*, with a clean conscience, as I hold you in unbroken remembrance in my petitions night and day,
\v 4 longing to see you — I remember your tears \add [i.e. the way you wept when we were parted]\add* — so that I may be filled full of joy,
\v 5 having been reminded of the faithfulness\f + \fr 1:5 \ft Greek \tl pistis\tl* holds both trust in God and fidelity to him; no one English word carries both, and this translation says "faithfulness" where fidelity is in view. "Without pretense" is \tl anypokritos\tl*, from the word for a stage actor in a mask: faith with no mask on it.\f* in you that is without pretense, which first made its home in your grandmother Lois and in your mother Eunice\f + \fr 1:5 \ft Timothy's mother was a Jewish believer and his father a Greek (Acts 16:1). The faith reached him down the women's side of the house, three generations of it.\f*, and I am persuaded that it is in you too.
\s1 Fan the Gift Back Into Flame
\p
\v 6 For this reason I remind you to fan back into flame\f + \fr 1:6 \ft Greek \tl anazōpyrein\tl*, to stir banked embers back to a blaze. The word assumes the fire is not out — only low and untended.\f* the gift of God that is in you through the laying on of my hands\f + \fr 1:6 \ft The commissioning is described at 1 Timothy 4:14 as the elders' hands laid on him; Paul's own hands were on him in the same act. The gesture is Moses laying hands on Joshua to hand over his charge (Numbers 27:18-23).\f*.
\v 7 For God did not give us a spirit of cowardice\f + \fr 1:7 \ft Greek \tl deilia\tl*. In Israel's scriptures the coward is the man sent home before the battle, lest his fear spread through the ranks (Deuteronomy 20:8; Judges 7:3).\f*, but of power and love and a sound mind \add [i.e. judgment that stays clear when fear would cloud it]\add*.
\s1 Do Not Be Ashamed — Take Your Share of the Hardship
\p
\v 8 So do not be ashamed of the testimony about our Lord \add [i.e. of standing up in public and saying openly whose you are]\add*, nor of me his prisoner \add [i.e. I am not Rome's prisoner but his; these chains came to me in his service]\add*; instead, take your share of hardship for the good news, according to the power of God,
\v 9 who rescued us and called us with a holy calling — not according to our works but according to his own purpose and favor, which was given us in the Christ Jesus before the times of the ages \add [i.e. before there was any time to count]\add*,
\v 10 and has now been made visible through the appearing\f + \fr 1:10 \ft Greek \tl epiphaneia\tl*, the word used when a god became visible or a king arrived in state. Here it is Christ's coming into the world; at 4:1 and 4:8 it is his coming back.\f* of our Rescuer, the Christ Jesus, who put death out of action \add [i.e. men still die, but death no longer has the last word]\add* and lit up life and incorruption \add [i.e. life that cannot decay or be taken back]\add* through the good news.
\v 11 For this good news I was appointed a herald \add [i.e. the man who stands in the square and reads out the king's decree]\add* and one sent and a teacher.
\v 12 This is the reason I also suffer these things; but I am not ashamed, for I know the one I have trusted, and I am persuaded that he is able to guard what I have placed in his keeping\f + \fr 1:12 \ft Greek \tl parathēkē\tl*, a deposit left with a trusted man for safekeeping, protected by law (Exodus 22:7-13). Here it runs one way: Paul has put his life and his work into God's hands. At 1:14 and 1 Timothy 6:20 it runs the other — the good news deposited with Timothy for him to keep intact.\f* until that day\f + \fr 1:12 \ft "That day" is the Day of the LORD of the prophets — the day of God's decisive judgment and vindication (Isaiah 2:12; Joel 2:31; Malachi 4:5). It returns at 1:18 and 4:8.\f*.
\s1 Guard What Was Put in Your Keeping
\p
\v 13 Hold to the pattern of the healthy words\f + \fr 1:13 \ft "Healthy" (Greek \tl hygiainein\tl*) is these letters' running figure for teaching: sound teaching makes a community well, and the other kind spreads through it like disease (2:17).\f* you heard from me, in the faithfulness and love that are in the Christ Jesus.
\v 14 Guard the good thing placed in your keeping, through the Holy Spirit who makes his home in us.
\s1 Those Who Turned Away, and the One Who Did Not
\p
\v 15 You know this: that all those in Asia \add [i.e. the province whose chief city is Ephesus, where you are standing]\add* turned their backs on me, among whom are Phygelus and Hermogenes.
\v 16 May the Lord grant mercy to the household of Onesiphorus, because he often revived me and was not ashamed of my chain \add [i.e. the chain that fastened me to my guard, and marked me to everyone as a man to be avoided]\add*.
\v 17 Instead, when he came to be in Rome \add [i.e. having made the whole journey from Asia]\add* he searched hard for me and found me —
\v 18 may the Lord grant him to find mercy from the Lord on that day\f + \fr 1:18 \ft Paul asks mercy for the household in the present and for the man himself on the last day. Whether Onesiphorus was still away from home or had since died, the letter does not say.\f*. And how much he served in Ephesus, you yourself know better than anyone.

\c 2
\s1 Hand It On to Men Who Will Hand It On
\p
\v 1 You then, my child, be given power in the favor that is in the Christ Jesus;
\v 2 and what you heard from me with many witnesses standing by \add [i.e. I taught you in the open, and there are men living who can say what I said]\add* — place that in the keeping of trustworthy men, such as will be able to teach others too.
\s1 Soldier, Athlete, Farmer
\p
\v 3 Take your share of hardship as a good soldier of the Christ Jesus.
\v 4 No man on campaign gets himself tangled in the business of daily living \add [i.e. a soldier on service does not also run a farm or a shop]\add*, so that he may please the one who enlisted him.
\v 5 And if anyone competes as an athlete, he is not crowned unless he competes by the rules\f + \fr 2:5 \ft At the great games an entrant swore an oath, completed a fixed training period, and was disqualified outright for violations. Winning by any other means won nothing.\f*.
\v 6 The farmer who does the hard work must be the first to take a share of the crops.
\v 7 Think over what I am saying; for the Lord will give you understanding in everything.
\s1 The Good News I Am Chained For
\p
\v 8 Keep remembering Jesus the Christ, raised from among the dead, from the seed of David\f + \fr 2:8 \ft The promise to David: "I will raise up your offspring after you... and I will establish the throne of his kingdom forever" (2 Samuel 7:12-13). Descent from David is what makes him Israel's promised king and not a saviour in general.\f*, according to my good news,
\v 9 for which I suffer hardship to the point of chains, like a criminal — but the word of God is not chained.
\v 10 For this reason I endure everything for the sake of the chosen ones\f + \fr 2:10 \ft Greek \tl eklektoi\tl*, the standing word in Israel's Greek scriptures for Israel itself as the people God took for himself — always a people, and always taken for a purpose (Isaiah 43:20; 45:4; Psalm 105:6).\f*, so that they too may obtain the rescue that is in the Christ Jesus, with everlasting glory.
\s1 A Saying You Can Rely On
\p
\v 11 Trustworthy is the word\f + \fr 2:11 \ft One of five sayings flagged this way in the letters to Timothy and Titus (1 Timothy 1:15; 3:1; 4:9; Titus 3:8). The balanced lines that follow suggest something already recited or sung in the assemblies, which Paul is quoting and endorsing.\f*: If we died together with him, we will also live together with him;
\v 12 if we endure, we will also reign together with him; if we deny him, that one will also deny us\f + \fr 2:12 \ft The line takes up a saying of Jesus: "whoever denies me before men, I also will deny before my Father" (Matthew 10:33; Luke 12:9).\f*;
\v 13 if we are faithless, that one stays faithful — for he cannot deny himself\f + \fr 2:13 \ft The last line does not cancel the one before it. What he stays faithful to is his own stated terms — the promises of the first two lines and the warning of the third alike. To fail either would be to go back on himself.\f*.
\s1 Cut Straight; Give the Word-Fighters a Wide Berth
\p
\v 14 Keep reminding them of these things, testifying solemnly before God that they are not to fight over words. It is good for nothing, and it overturns those who hear.
\v 15 Make it your work to present yourself to God as one who has passed the test, a workman with nothing to be ashamed of, cutting a straight line through the word of the truth\f + \fr 2:15 \ft Greek \tl orthotomein\tl*, to cut straight — as a mason cuts a stone true, or a road-builder drives his road through the terrain instead of wandering round it.\f*.
\v 16 Give the profane empty noises a wide berth, for they will advance further into irreverence,
\v 17 and their word will eat its way outward like gangrene. Among them are Hymenaeus and Philetus,
\v 18 who have missed the mark concerning the truth, saying that the rising has already happened \add [i.e. that the raising of the dead is behind us and done — so there is nothing left to wait for, and nothing to endure for]\add*, and they are overturning the faith of some.
\v 19 God's solid foundation stands, all the same, and it carries this seal \add [i.e. an inscription cut into the foundation stone, the way a builder puts his name on what he has laid]\add*: "The Lord knew those who are his," and, "Let everyone who names the name of the Lord stand away from unrighteousness."\f + \fr 2:19 \ft Both lines come from the Korah rebellion (Numbers 16). Challenged over who truly belonged to God, Moses answered, "God will make known those who are his" (16:5), and then, "Stand away from the tents of these men" (16:26) — after which the ground opened under them. The comfort in the first line and the warning in the second belong to the same story.\f*
\s1 Vessels in a Great House
\p
\v 20 In a great house there are not only vessels of gold and silver but also of wood and clay — some for honor, some for dishonor.
\v 21 So if anyone scours himself clean of these \add [i.e. of the dishonorable things — the empty talk, the word-fights, the wrong]\add*, he will be a vessel for honor, made holy, useful to the master of the house, made ready for every good work.
\s1 How the Lord's Slave Deals With Opponents
\p
\v 22 Flee the cravings of youth \add [i.e. the appetite for an argument, for being thought clever, for whatever is newest]\add*; chase after righteousness, faithfulness, love, peace, together with those who call on the Lord out of a clean heart.
\v 23 Have nothing to do with foolish and untaught questionings; you know that they breed fights.
\v 24 And a slave of the Lord must not fight, but be gentle toward everyone, able to teach, able to bear being wronged,
\v 25 training with gentleness those who set themselves against him — in case God may grant them a turning back, leading to a full knowing of the truth,
\v 26 and they may sober up again out of the devil's snare, taken alive by him as they have been, for that one's will \add [i.e. the devil's will; they are serving it whether they know it or not]\add*.

\c 3
\s1 What the Last Days Are Like
\p
\v 1 Know this: that in the last days\f + \fr 3:1 \ft The prophets' phrase for the age in which God's purposes come to a head (Isaiah 2:2; Micah 4:1; Daniel 2:28). The first assemblies understood that age to have already begun with Christ (Acts 2:17; Hebrews 1:2) — which is why verse 5 can tell Timothy to turn away from such men. They are already at hand.\f* hard seasons will set in.
\v 2 For men will be lovers of themselves, lovers of money, braggarts, arrogant, blasphemers, disobedient to parents, ungrateful, unholy,
\v 3 without natural affection, refusing to make a truce, slanderers, with no mastery of themselves, savage, with no love for good,
\v 4 betrayers, headlong, puffed up, lovers of pleasure rather than lovers of God,
\v 5 holding the shape of reverence for God while denying its power \add [i.e. the whole outward form is in place — these are men inside the assembly, not outside it — but the power that actually changes a man has been shown the door]\add*. Turn away from these men.
\s1 How These Men Work, and How They End
\p
\v 6 For from among these come the ones who worm their way into houses \add [i.e. the households where the assembly meets]\add* and take captive weak little women\f + \fr 3:6 \ft The Greek is a diminutive, \tl gynaikarion\tl*. What follows in the verse is what makes these particular women easy prey — a load of unresolved guilt and appetites pulling every direction — not a verdict on women generally.\f*, heaped up with sins, driven along by cravings of every kind,
\v 7 always learning and never able to arrive at a full knowing of the truth.
\v 8 In the way that Jannes and Jambres stood against Moses\f + \fr 3:8 \ft The names are not in Exodus; Jewish tradition supplied them for Pharaoh's magicians, who matched Moses sign for sign until the point where they could not, and then were struck with the same boils as everyone else (Exodus 7:11-22; 8:18-19; 9:11).\f*, so these also stand against the truth — men with ruined minds, who have failed the test concerning the faith.
\v 9 But they will not advance much further, for their mindlessness will become plain to everyone, as the mindlessness of those two did.
\s1 But You Have Watched Me Up Close
\p
\v 10 But you have followed closely my teaching, my way of life, my purpose, my faithfulness, my long-suffering, my love, my endurance,
\v 11 my persecutions, my sufferings — the kind that came on me in Antioch, in Iconium, in Lystra\f + \fr 3:11 \ft Acts 13:50-14:20. At Lystra Paul was stoned and dragged out of the city for dead. Lystra is Timothy's own town (Acts 16:1): he chose to follow Paul having seen with his own eyes what following him cost.\f*. What persecutions I bore up under! And out of them all the Lord rescued me.
\v 12 And indeed all who want to live with reverence for God in the Christ Jesus will be persecuted,
\v 13 while evil men and frauds will advance toward the worse, leading astray and being led astray \add [i.e. they are inside the deception themselves, which is why nothing learned from them ever arrives anywhere]\add*.
\s1 Stay in What You Learned — the Writings Will Equip You
\p
\v 14 But you, stay in what you learned and were made sure of, knowing from whom you learned it \add [i.e. from me, and from your mother and grandmother before me — people whose faithfulness you have watched]\add*,
\v 15 and that from infancy you have known the holy writings \add [i.e. Israel's scriptures, taught you at home before you could read them yourself]\add*, which are able to make you wise for rescue through faithfulness in the Christ Jesus.
\v 16 Every Scripture is breathed out by God\f + \fr 3:16 \ft Greek \tl theopneustos\tl*, a word found nowhere else in these writings and almost nowhere in Greek before them. God's breath is what makes and what speaks: it gives Adam life (Genesis 2:7), it makes the heavens (Psalm 33:6), it puts words in a prophet's mouth (2 Samuel 23:2; Isaiah 59:21). The writings meant are the ones named in the previous verse — Israel's.\f* and useful for teaching, for exposing what is wrong, for setting straight again, for training in righteousness,
\v 17 so that the man of God\f + \fr 3:17 \ft A title from Israel's scriptures for a man who stands in God's service and speaks on his behalf — Moses, Elijah, Elisha, David (Deuteronomy 33:1; 1 Kings 17:18; 2 Kings 4:9). Timothy is being placed in that line.\f* may be complete, fully outfitted for every good work.

\c 4
\s1 Herald the Word, in Season and Out
\p
\v 1 I testify solemnly before God and the Christ Jesus, who is about to judge living and dead, and by his appearing and his kingdom \add [i.e. by the day he shows himself and takes up his rule in the open]\add*:
\v 2 herald the word; be at hand in season and out of season \add [i.e. when they want to hear it and when they do not]\add*; expose, rebuke, urge on — with all long-suffering and teaching.
\v 3 For there will be a season when they will not put up with the healthy teaching; instead, to suit their own cravings, they will heap up teachers for themselves \add [i.e. collect one after another, until they have a stack of voices all saying what they wanted said]\add*, with ears that itch to be scratched,
\v 4 and they will turn their hearing away from the truth and be turned aside into the tales \add [i.e. the invented stories that give a man something to think about and nothing to obey]\add*.
\v 5 But you, be sober in everything; suffer hardship; do the work of one who announces good news; carry your service through to the full.
\s1 My Own Race Is Finished
\p
\v 6 For I am already being poured out as a drink offering\f + \fr 4:6 \ft The libation of wine poured out alongside the daily sacrifice (Numbers 15:1-10; 28:7). It was not eaten or kept back by anyone: it went onto the altar and was gone.\f*, and the season of my loosing \add [i.e. of casting off, as a ship is untied from its moorings]\add* stands at hand.
\v 7 I have contended in the good contest, I have finished the course, I have kept the faith.
\v 8 From here on the crown of righteousness\f + \fr 4:8 \ft Greek \tl stephanos\tl*, the victor's wreath at the games, not a king's diadem. The athlete of 2:5 who competed by the rules gets one; so does the man who finishes the course.\f* is laid away for me, which the Lord will give back to me on that day — he is the righteous judge — and not to me only, but to all who have loved his appearing.
\s1 Come to Me, and Bring These
\p
\v 9 Make every effort to come to me quickly.
\v 10 For Demas left me behind, having loved the present age \add [i.e. this world as it now stands — the very thing that the appearing will bring to an end]\add*, and went to Thessalonica; Crescens to Galatia, Titus to Dalmatia.
\v 11 Luke alone is with me. Pick up Mark and bring him with you, for he is useful to me for service\f + \fr 4:11 \ft The same Mark who abandoned the first journey at Pamphylia, and over whom Paul and Barnabas quarrelled so sharply that they separated (Acts 13:13; 15:37-39). He is wanted now.\f*.
\v 12 Tychicus I sent to Ephesus.
\v 13 When you come, bring the cloak \add [i.e. the heavy travelling cloak; winter is coming and this place is cold]\add* I left behind at Troas with Carpus, and the scrolls — above all the parchments\f + \fr 4:13 \ft Papyrus rolls, and sheets of prepared skin, which cost more and lasted longer. What was on them the letter does not say.\f*.
\s1 Watch Out for Alexander
\p
\v 14 Alexander the coppersmith\f + \fr 4:14 \ft Possibly the Alexander of 1 Timothy 1:20, handed over to Satan with Hymenaeus so that he would learn not to blaspheme. If so, the correction did not take.\f* showed me much harm. The Lord will give back to him according to his works.
\v 15 Guard yourself against him too, for he stood hard against our words.
\s1 No One Stood With Me — the Lord Did
\p
\v 16 At my first defense \add [i.e. the first hearing of my case; a Roman trial ran in stages]\add* no one came to stand with me \add [i.e. no advocate, no character witness, no one at all to show the court that I had friends]\add*; they all left me behind. May it not be counted against them.
\v 17 But the Lord stood by me and put power in me, so that through me the heralding might be carried through to the full and all the nations might hear. And I was rescued out of the lion's mouth\f + \fr 4:17 \ft "Save me from the mouth of the lion" (Psalm 22:21) — the cry of the suffering righteous man. Here the lion is the Roman court, and the rescue is that the first hearing did not end in a sentence.\f*.
\v 18 The Lord will rescue me out of every evil work and will bring me safely into his heavenly kingdom \add [i.e. not a rescue from dying — that is already settled — but a rescue through it]\add*. To him be the glory into the ages of the ages. Amen.
\s1 Final Greetings
\p
\v 19 Greet Prisca and Aquila\f + \fr 4:19 \ft The tentmaking couple Paul lodged and worked with at Corinth, who then travelled with him to Ephesus and hosted an assembly in their house (Acts 18:2-3, 18-19, 26; Romans 16:3-5).\f*, and the household of Onesiphorus.
\v 20 Erastus stayed in Corinth; Trophimus I left behind sick at Miletus.
\v 21 Make every effort to come before winter \add [i.e. once the sailing season shuts you will not reach me until spring]\add*. Eubulus greets you, and Pudens and Linus and Claudia and all the brothers.
\v 22 The Lord be with your spirit. Favor be with you\f + \fr 4:22 \ft "You" here is plural, where everything before it has been singular. The letter is Timothy's, but it was meant to be read out where the assembly could hear it.\f*.
